":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You do. Most policies only require that each side’s appraiser be competent and disinterested, so someone with a stake in the repair contract may draw an objection."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can my insurer ignore a written appraisal demand?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"If the policy has an appraisal clause and the dispute is about the amount of loss, insurers generally participate once a proper demand is made. Coverage disputes are different, so check your policy and consider legal advice."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is the award final?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"An award signed by two of the three panel members is generally binding on the amount of loss, subject to limited court review. It does not by itself resolve coverage questions."}}],"@id":"https://newyorkinsuranceappraiser.com/claim-appraisals/appraisal-panel-representation/#faq"},{"@type":"Service","name":"Appraisal Clause Representation","serviceType":"Appraisal Clause Representation","description":"Appraisal clause representation for New York property claims: we serve as your named appraiser, rebuild the scope and work toward a signed award.","provider":{"@id":"https://newyorkinsuranceappraiser.com/#business"},"areaServed":{"@type":"State","name":"New York"},"url":"https://newyorkinsuranceappraiser.com/claim-appraisals/appraisal-panel-representation/","image":"https://newyorkinsuranceappraiser.com/assets/img/photos/consult.webp","@id":"https://newyorkinsuranceappraiser.com/claim-appraisals/appraisal-panel-representation/#service"}]}</script>
