<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Where the New York appraisal clause comes from</h2>
<p>Good <strong>appraisal clause representation</strong> starts with knowing the language. The appraisal paragraph in most U.S. property policies traces back to the 1943 New York Standard Fire Policy, codified in Insurance Law §3404. The mechanics are short: a written demand, each side chooses a competent and disinterested appraiser, the two appraisers pick an umpire, and a written agreement by any two of the three sets the amount of loss. If the appraisers cannot agree on an umpire, a judge can generally be asked to appoint one.</p>
<p>Modern homeowners, condo and commercial forms reword that paragraph, so we always read your actual policy before the demand goes out. Our guide to the <a href="/policyholder-guides/new-york-standard-fire-policy-appraisal-clause/">New York Standard Fire Policy appraisal clause</a> walks through the wording line by line.</p>
<h2>What sitting in your seat involves</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Policy and deadline review.</strong> We check the appraisal wording, the suit-limitation period (many New York policies use two years from the loss) and any notice requirements.</li>
<li><strong>An independent scope.</strong> We inspect and measure the property ourselves instead of marking up the carrier’s estimate.</li>
<li><strong>Local pricing.</strong> A Buffalo double and a Park Slope brownstone do not cost the same to repair; we price labor and materials for the actual market.</li>
<li><strong>Working sessions with the other appraiser.</strong> Most disagreements narrow line by line before an umpire is ever involved.</li>
<li><strong>Umpire selection and presentation</strong> for whatever items remain open.</li>
<li><strong>Award review</strong> so replacement cost, actual cash value and any depreciation holdback are clearly stated.</li>
</ul>
<p>From you, we generally need the full policy with endorsements, the insurer’s estimate and payment letters, photos from the day of the loss, and any contractor bids. Once both appraisers are named, a straightforward residential panel can move quickly, while a large commercial loss or a heavily contested contents claim takes longer. We keep you informed at each stage, explain every concession before it is made, and never sign an award you have not seen. You stay in control of the claim; our job is to give the panel a valuation that stands up to scrutiny.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Scope of appraisal</b>An appraisal panel values the loss. It generally does not decide coverage questions, exclusions or claims-handling conduct. If your claim was denied outright, talk to a New York attorney before treating appraisal as the answer.</div>
<h2>Is your dispute a good fit?</h2>
<p>Appraisal tends to work when the insurer accepts the claim but the numbers are far apart: how many roof slopes need replacing, whether hardwood can be matched, what a plaster ceiling in a pre-war Manhattan co-op really costs. It is a weaker tool when the real fight is wear and tear, late notice or a flood exclusion. Our <a href="/appraisal-calculators/appraisal-readiness-checkup/">appraisal readiness checkup</a> can help you sort that out first.</p>
<h2>Independence matters on a New York panel</h2>
<p>The clause asks for a “disinterested” appraiser. We do not take a share of the repair contract, and we tell you honestly what an umpire is likely to accept. If you are still deciding between an appraiser, a DFS-licensed public adjuster or a lawyer, see <a href="/policyholder-guides/appraiser-public-adjuster-or-attorney-new-york/">who does what in New York</a>.</p>

<h2>Questions about appraisal clause representation</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who chooses my appraiser in New York?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>You do. Most policies only require that each side’s appraiser be competent and disinterested, so someone with a stake in the repair contract may draw an objection.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can my insurer ignore a written appraisal demand?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>If the policy has an appraisal clause and the dispute is about the amount of loss, insurers generally participate once a proper demand is made. Coverage disputes are different, so check your policy and consider legal advice.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is the award final?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>An award signed by two of the three panel members is generally binding on the amount of loss, subject to limited court review. It does not by itself resolve coverage questions.</p></div></details></div>
